__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"PROPHETS_A",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'adam',name:'Prophet Adam',arabic:'\u0622\u062f\u0645 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The first human',minutes:8,color:'#4E6B3A',paragraphs:['Before there were any people on earth, Allah told the angels that He was going to place a khalifah, a successor, on the earth. The angels asked, "Will You place on it someone who will spread corruption and shed blood, while we glorify You and praise You?" Allah answered, "I know what you do not know."'],sections:[{heading:'Made from clay, taught the names',paragraphs:['Allah created Adam from clay, shaped him with His own hands, and breathed into him a soul He created. Adam became the first human being, the father of every person who has ever lived.','Then Allah honoured Adam with knowledge. He taught him the names of all things. Allah showed these things to the angels and said, "Tell Me their names, if you are right." The angels said, "Glory be to You. We have no knowledge except what You have taught us." Then Allah said, "O Adam, tell them their names," and Adam did. The angels saw that this new creation had been given something special.'],refs:['Quran 2:30-33','Quran 15:26-29','Quran 38:71-75']},{heading:'The pride of Iblis',paragraphs:['Allah commanded the angels to bow down to Adam as a mark of honour. Every one of them bowed, except Iblis. Iblis was from the jinn, created from smokeless fire, and he had been among the angels in worship.','Allah asked him, "What stopped you from bowing when I commanded you?" Iblis said, "I am better than him. You created me from fire and created him from clay." His pride made him refuse, and he was thrown out of Allah\'s mercy. Instead of asking forgiveness, Iblis asked to be given time until the Day of Judgement, and swore that he would mislead Adam\'s children from every direction, from the front, from behind, from the right and from the left. Allah told him that he would have no power over Allah\'s sincere servants.'],refs:['Quran 7:11-18','Quran 15:30-42','Quran 18:50']},{heading:'The Garden and the tree',paragraphs:['Allah created Hawwa as a wife and companion for Adam, and told them to live in the Garden and eat freely from it, but not to come near one particular tree. Allah warned Adam that Shaytan was a clear enemy to him and his wife.','Shaytan began to whisper. He told them that the tree would make them like angels or let them live forever, and he swore to them that he was only giving sincere advice. Little by little he led them on, until they both ate from the tree. Straight away they became aware of themselves and began to cover themselves with the leaves of the Garden.'],refs:['Quran 2:35-36','Quran 7:19-22','Quran 20:115-121']},{heading:'Turning back to Allah',paragraphs:['Adam and Hawwa did not argue or blame anyone else. They were truly sorry. Allah taught Adam words to say, and they prayed: "Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will surely be among the losers."','Allah forgave them, because He is the One who accepts repentance, the Most Merciful. Then He sent them down to the earth to live, and promised that guidance would come to their children. Whoever follows that guidance will have no fear and will not grieve.','This is the difference between Adam and Iblis. Both disobeyed. Iblis was proud and blamed Allah. Adam was humble and asked forgiveness. That is why the door of repentance is open for every one of Adam\'s children.'],refs:['Quran 2:37-38','Quran 7:23-25','Quran 20:122-123']},{heading:'The two sons of Adam',paragraphs:['Adam and Hawwa had children, and the earth began to fill. The Quran tells of two of Adam\'s sons who each offered a sacrifice to Allah. It was accepted from one and not from the other. The one whose offering was rejected became jealous and said, "I will kill you." His brother answered, "Allah only accepts from those who are mindful of Him. Even if you stretch out your hand to kill me, I will not stretch out mine to kill you, because I fear Allah."','The jealous brother killed him, and then did not know what to do with the body. Allah sent a crow that dug in the ground to show him how to bury it. He cried, "Woe to me! Could I not even be like this crow?" and he was filled with regret. After this, Allah made it clear that killing one innocent soul is like killing all of humanity, and saving one life is like saving all of humanity.'],refs:['Quran 5:27-32']}],lesson:'Everyone makes mistakes. The best people do not blame others; they turn back to Allah quickly and ask forgiveness. Pride ruins a person, humility raises them.',question:'Adam and Iblis both disobeyed Allah. What did each of them do next, and why did it matter?',refs:['Quran 2:30-38','Quran 7:11-25','Quran 20:115-123','Quran 5:27-32']},{id:'idris',name:'Prophet Idris',arabic:'\u0625\u062f\u0631\u064a\u0633 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Raised to a high place',minutes:3,color:'#7A3E6E',paragraphs:['Idris was one of the earliest prophets, coming after Adam and Shith and before Nuh. The Quran mentions him only briefly, but with great honour.'],sections:[{heading:'A man of truth',paragraphs:['Allah says: "And mention in the Book, Idris. He was a man of truth and a prophet. And We raised him to a high place." Allah also counts him among the patient: "And Ismail, Idris and Dhul-Kifl, all were among the patient. We admitted them into Our mercy. They were among the righteous."','Being called "a man of truth" (siddiq) is one of the highest praises in the Quran. It means his words, his actions and his heart were all true to Allah.'],refs:['Quran 19:56-57','Quran 21:85-86']},{heading:'Met on the night journey',paragraphs:['On the night of the Isra and Miraj, the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa was taken up through the heavens. In the fourth heaven he met Idris, who greeted him, saying, "Welcome to the righteous brother and the righteous prophet." This is reported in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim.','Some scholars mention that Idris was among the first to write with a pen and to teach people useful skills, but these reports are not as strong as the Quran and authentic hadith. What we know for certain is that Allah loved him, raised his rank, and made him an example of truthfulness and patience.'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 3207','Sahih Muslim 162']}],lesson:'Allah raises people because of their truthfulness and patience, not because of fame. A few words from Allah about someone can be worth more than a whole book of praise from people.',question:'What two qualities does the Quran praise Idris for?',refs:['Quran 19:56-57','Quran 21:85-86']},{id:'nuh',name:'Prophet Nuh',arabic:'\u0646\u0648\u062d \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The Ark',minutes:8,color:'#1F5F7A',paragraphs:['Many generations after Adam, people began to make statues of righteous men who had passed away, at first only to remember them. Over time, later generations started to worship those statues. Allah sent Nuh to bring his people back to worshipping Allah alone.'],sections:[{heading:'950 years of calling',paragraphs:['Nuh stayed among his people for 950 years, calling them to Allah. He said, "My people, worship Allah. You have no god other than Him. I fear for you the punishment of a terrible Day."','He tried every way. He called them at night and by day, in public and in private. He told them, "Ask your Lord for forgiveness, He is always forgiving. He will send down rain for you, give you wealth and children, and give you gardens and rivers." But every time he called them, they put their fingers in their ears, covered themselves with their clothes and turned away in pride.','The leaders mocked him: "We only see you as a man like us, and only the lowest of us follow you." They told him to drive the poor believers away. Nuh refused: "I will not drive away the believers. They will meet their Lord."'],refs:['Quran 29:14','Quran 71:5-12','Quran 11:25-31']},{heading:'Building the Ark',paragraphs:['After all those years, Allah told Nuh that no more of his people would believe, except those who already had. Nuh prayed, "My Lord, I am overpowered, so help me." Allah commanded him to build a great ship, the Ark, under His watch and guidance.','Nuh built it on dry land, far from any sea. Whenever the chiefs walked past, they laughed at him. Nuh replied, "If you laugh at us now, we will laugh at you in the same way, and you will come to know who will receive a humiliating punishment."'],refs:['Quran 11:36-39','Quran 54:9-13','Quran 23:27']},{heading:'The flood',paragraphs:['When Allah\'s command came, water burst from the earth and poured from the sky. Allah told Nuh to take on board a pair of every kind of animal, his family except those who had rejected, and all the believers. They were only a few. Nuh said, "Board it. In the name of Allah it sails and in His name it comes to rest. My Lord is Forgiving and Merciful."','The Ark sailed on waves like mountains. Nuh saw his own son standing apart and called out, "My son, come aboard with us, and do not be with the disbelievers." His son answered, "I will go up a mountain to protect me from the water." Nuh said, "Today there is no protection from Allah\'s command except for those He has mercy on." Then a wave came between them, and his son was among those who drowned.','Nuh asked Allah about his son, saying his son was from his family. Allah told him that his son was not of his family in faith, because of what he had chosen. Nuh at once asked Allah\'s forgiveness for asking about something he had no knowledge of.'],refs:['Quran 11:40-47','Quran 54:11-12','Quran 66:10']},{heading:'A new beginning',paragraphs:['Then it was said: "O earth, swallow your water, and O sky, hold back." The water went down, the matter was settled, and the Ark came to rest on Mount Judi.','Nuh and the believers came out with peace and blessings from Allah, and from them the earth was filled with people again. Allah made the Ark a sign for all who come after, and He left this praise of Nuh among later generations: "Peace be upon Nuh among all the worlds."'],refs:['Quran 11:44-48','Quran 29:15','Quran 37:75-79']}],lesson:'Keep calling to what is good with patience, even when people mock you. Being the son of a prophet did not save Nuh\'s son; only faith saves.',question:'What did Nuh\'s son think would save him from the flood? What did Nuh tell him?',refs:['Quran 11:25-49','Quran 71','Quran 29:14-15','Quran 54:9-16']},{id:'hud',name:'Prophet Hud',arabic:'\u0647\u0648\u062f \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The people of \'Ad',minutes:5,color:'#A8573A',paragraphs:['After the flood, people spread across the earth. In the south of Arabia, in a land of sand hills called al-Ahqaf, lived the people of \'Ad. They were tall and very strong, and Allah had given them great wealth, cattle, children, gardens and springs. Allah sent them one of their own, Hud.'],sections:[{heading:'A strong nation that forgot',paragraphs:['\'Ad built tall towers on every high place just to show off, and great fortresses as if they would live forever. When they attacked others, they did it with cruelty. They worshipped idols and said, "Who is stronger than us?" They forgot that Allah, who created them, was far stronger.','Hud said to them, "My people, worship Allah. You have no god but Him. I ask you for no payment; my reward is only from the One who created me. Ask your Lord for forgiveness and turn to Him. He will send you abundant rain and add strength to your strength."'],refs:['Quran 7:65-69','Quran 11:50-52','Quran 26:123-135','Quran 41:15','Quran 89:6-8']},{heading:'Hud stands alone',paragraphs:['They answered, "You have brought us no clear proof. We will not leave our gods because you say so. We think one of our gods has struck you with madness."','Hud stood alone against a whole nation and said, "I call Allah to witness, and you be witness too, that I am free of what you worship besides Him. So plot against me, all of you, and give me no time. I have put my trust in Allah, my Lord and your Lord. There is no living creature except that He holds it by its forelock."'],refs:['Quran 11:53-57']},{heading:'The wind',paragraphs:['A drought came. Then one day they saw a dark cloud moving towards their valleys and rejoiced: "This cloud will bring us rain!" Hud told them, "No, it is what you were asking to be hurried: a wind carrying a painful punishment."','Allah sent upon them a furious, freezing wind for seven nights and eight days without stopping. It flung people down like hollow trunks of palm trees. Afterwards nothing could be seen of them except their empty homes. Allah saved Hud and those who believed with him by His mercy.'],refs:['Quran 46:21-25','Quran 69:6-8','Quran 11:58']}],lesson:'Strength, money and buildings mean nothing without faith. Never say "Who is stronger than us?"; Allah is always stronger.',question:'What did the people of \'Ad think the dark cloud was? What was it really?',refs:['Quran 7:65-72','Quran 11:50-60','Quran 26:123-140','Quran 46:21-26','Quran 69:6-8']},{id:'salih',name:'Prophet Salih',arabic:'\u0635\u0627\u0644\u062d \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The she-camel of Allah',minutes:5,color:'#9E3F5C',paragraphs:['After \'Ad came the people of Thamud, who lived in the north of Arabia in a place called al-Hijr. They carved beautiful homes out of the mountains and built palaces on the plains. Allah sent them their brother Salih, a man they had once trusted and respected.'],sections:[{heading:'A call to give thanks',paragraphs:['Salih said, "My people, worship Allah. You have no god but Him. He created you from the earth and settled you in it. So ask His forgiveness and turn to Him. My Lord is near and answers prayer."','They said, "Salih, we had high hopes for you before this. Do you forbid us to worship what our fathers worshipped?" They asked him for a miracle to prove he was a messenger.'],refs:['Quran 11:61-62','Quran 7:73-74','Quran 15:80-82']},{heading:'The she-camel',paragraphs:['Allah sent them a she-camel as a clear sign. Salih told them, "This is the she-camel of Allah, a sign for you. Let her graze freely on Allah\'s earth and do her no harm." The water was to be shared: one day for her to drink, and one day for the people.','For a while they left her alone. But the arrogant leaders hated the sign. The most wretched of them was sent to kill her, and he hamstrung her while the others agreed. Then they mocked Salih: "Bring us the punishment you threaten us with, if you are a messenger."'],refs:['Quran 7:73','Quran 26:154-157','Quran 54:27-29','Quran 91:11-14']},{heading:'Three days',paragraphs:['Salih told them, "Enjoy yourselves in your homes for three days. That is a promise that will not be broken." Nine troublemakers in the city even plotted to kill Salih and his family at night, but Allah protected him.','Then a mighty blast and an earthquake seized them, and in the morning they lay dead in their homes as if they had never lived there. Allah saved Salih and the believers. Centuries later, when the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa passed by the ruins of Thamud on the way to Tabuk, he told his companions to pass through humbly and in tears, and not to use the water of their wells.'],refs:['Quran 11:65-68','Quran 27:48-53','Sahih al-Bukhari 4419']}],lesson:'When Allah gives you a blessing, protect it and be thankful. Agreeing with someone else\'s wrongdoing makes you share in it: the whole people were punished because they approved.',question:'What was the sign Allah sent to Thamud, and what rule came with it?',refs:['Quran 7:73-79','Quran 11:61-68','Quran 26:141-159','Quran 91:11-15']},{id:'ibrahim',name:'Prophet Ibrahim',arabic:'\u0625\u0628\u0631\u0627\u0647\u064a\u0645 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The friend of Allah',minutes:12,color:'#C9703A',paragraphs:['Ibrahim is one of the greatest prophets. Allah calls him His khalil, His close friend, and calls him "a nation" all by himself. Jews, Christians and Muslims all honour him, and every Muslim sends blessings on him in every prayer.'],sections:[{heading:'Searching for the truth',paragraphs:['Ibrahim grew up in a land where people worshipped idols and the stars. His own father, Azar, made idols. Even as a young man Ibrahim knew that something that cannot hear, see or help could not be God.','He taught his people with a clever lesson. When he saw a bright star at night, he said, "This is my lord?" When it set, he said, "I do not love things that set." He did the same with the moon, and then with the sun, the biggest of all. When the sun set too, he said, "My people, I am free of what you worship. I turn my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth, and I am not one of those who worship others with Him."'],refs:['Quran 6:74-79','Quran 4:125','Quran 16:120']},{heading:'Gentle with his father',paragraphs:['Ibrahim spoke to his father with great respect: "My dear father, why do you worship something that cannot hear or see or help you at all? My dear father, knowledge has come to me that has not come to you, so follow me and I will guide you to a straight path."','His father threatened to stone him and told him to leave. Ibrahim answered, "Peace be upon you. I will ask my Lord to forgive you. He has always been kind to me." Even when he was thrown out, he did not answer rudeness with rudeness.'],refs:['Quran 19:41-48']},{heading:'Breaking the idols',paragraphs:['One day the people went out to a festival. Ibrahim stayed behind, went into the temple and broke all the idols into pieces, except the biggest one. When the people returned and found their gods smashed, they asked, "Who did this?" Some said, "We heard a young man called Ibrahim speak about them."','They brought him before everyone and asked, "Did you do this to our gods, Ibrahim?" He said, "No, this biggest one did it. Ask them, if they can speak." For a moment they knew the truth and said to themselves, "You are the wrongdoers." Then they hung their heads: "You know these do not speak." Ibrahim said, "Then do you worship, instead of Allah, things that cannot help you or harm you at all?"'],refs:['Quran 21:51-67','Quran 37:88-96']},{heading:'The fire',paragraphs:['Instead of accepting the truth, they said, "Burn him and stand up for your gods." They built an enormous fire and threw Ibrahim into it with a catapult. As he was thrown, he said, "Allah is enough for us, and He is the best One to rely on."','Allah commanded: "O fire, be cool and safe for Ibrahim." The fire burned only the ropes that tied him, and Ibrahim walked out unhurt. Their plot came to nothing.'],refs:['Quran 21:68-70','Sahih al-Bukhari 4563']},{heading:'Arguing with the king',paragraphs:['Ibrahim also stood before a proud king who claimed to have power over life and death. Ibrahim said, "My Lord is the One who gives life and causes death." The king said, "I give life and cause death," meaning he could pardon or execute people.','So Ibrahim said, "Allah brings the sun up from the east. Bring it up from the west." The king was left speechless.'],refs:['Quran 2:258']},{heading:'Hajar, Ismail and Zamzam',paragraphs:['Ibrahim left his homeland, trusting Allah. When he was old, Allah gave him a son, Ismail, from his wife Hajar. By Allah\'s command, Ibrahim took Hajar and baby Ismail to a dry valley with no water and no people: the valley of Makkah. When Hajar asked, "Did Allah command you to do this?" and Ibrahim said yes, she replied, "Then He will not let us be lost."','When their water ran out, Hajar ran back and forth seven times between the hills of Safa and Marwa, looking for help. Then the angel Jibril struck the ground and the water of Zamzam burst out. Pilgrims still walk between Safa and Marwa today in her memory, and Zamzam still flows.','Ibrahim prayed for that valley: "My Lord, make this land safe, and keep me and my children away from worshipping idols. Our Lord, I have settled some of my family in a valley without crops, by Your Sacred House, so that they establish prayer. Make people\'s hearts long for them, and provide them with fruits so that they may be thankful."'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 3364','Quran 14:35-41','Quran 2:158']},{heading:'The great test',paragraphs:['When Ismail was old enough to walk and work with him, Ibrahim saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son. Dreams of prophets are revelation. He told Ismail, "My son, I have seen in a dream that I am sacrificing you. What do you think?" Ismail answered, "My father, do what you are commanded. You will find me, if Allah wills, among the patient."','When they had both submitted and Ibrahim laid his son down, Allah called out, "O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision." Allah ransomed Ismail with a great sacrifice, a ram. Every Eid al-Adha, Muslims remember this moment of complete trust when they offer their own sacrifice.'],refs:['Quran 37:99-111']},{heading:'Building the Kaaba',paragraphs:['Years later, Ibrahim and Ismail raised the foundations of the Kaaba together, the first house of worship for Allah on earth. Ismail brought the stones and Ibrahim built, and as they worked they prayed: "Our Lord, accept this from us. You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing."','Ibrahim also asked Allah to send a messenger from among the people of Makkah who would recite Allah\'s verses to them, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them. That prayer was answered with the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa, a descendant of Ismail. Then Allah told Ibrahim to call all people to Hajj, and pilgrims have answered that call ever since.'],refs:['Quran 2:124-129','Quran 22:26-27','Quran 3:96-97']}],lesson:'Trust Allah completely, even when His command seems hard. Ibrahim was gentle with his father, brave with his people and obedient to his Lord, and Allah turned fire into safety and a dry valley into the heart of the Muslim world.',question:'How did Ibrahim show his people that their idols could not be gods?',refs:['Quran 2:124-131','Quran 6:74-83','Quran 14:35-41','Quran 19:41-50','Quran 21:51-73','Quran 37:83-113']},{id:'lut',name:'Prophet Lut',arabic:'\u0644\u0648\u0637 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Standing up for what is right',minutes:5,color:'#4E5A8C',paragraphs:['Lut was the nephew of Ibrahim and believed in him early on. Allah sent him to the people of Sodom and the towns around it, near the Dead Sea. They had invented a shameful sin that no people before them had committed, they robbed travellers on the roads, and they did evil openly in their gatherings.'],sections:[{heading:'Lut warns his people',paragraphs:['Lut said to them, "Will you not fear Allah? I am a trustworthy messenger to you. I ask no payment from you." He told them to stop their shameful acts and to come back to what Allah had made pure and lawful for them.','Instead of listening, they said, "Throw Lut and his family out of your town. They are people who want to keep themselves pure!" They mocked him for being clean.'],refs:['Quran 7:80-82','Quran 26:160-168','Quran 29:28-29']},{heading:'The guests',paragraphs:['Allah sent angels in the form of handsome young men. They visited Ibrahim first and gave him the good news of a son, and told him they were going to the people of Lut. Then they came to Lut as guests. He was worried for them, because he knew what his people were like.','The men of the town rushed to his house. Lut begged them, "My people, fear Allah and do not disgrace me with my guests. Is there not one sensible man among you?" They would not listen. Then the guests told him, "O Lut, we are messengers of your Lord. They will never reach you. Leave with your family in the last part of the night, and let none of you look back, except your wife."'],refs:['Quran 11:69-81','Quran 15:61-66']},{heading:'The morning',paragraphs:['Lut and the believers in his family left at night. His wife stayed with the wrongdoers; she had betrayed him by siding with them. At sunrise the punishment came: Allah turned the towns upside down and rained stones of baked clay on them.','Allah left a clear sign of this for people who think. Lut is an example of a man who stood almost alone, but never stopped speaking the truth or protecting his guests.'],refs:['Quran 11:81-83','Quran 15:73-77','Quran 66:10']}],lesson:'Speak up for what is right, even if you are the only one. Being related to a good person does not save someone; Lut\'s own wife was lost because of her choices.',question:'What did the people of Lut mock him and his family for?',refs:['Quran 7:80-84','Quran 11:77-83','Quran 15:57-77','Quran 26:160-175','Quran 29:28-35']},{id:'ismail',name:'Prophet Ismail',arabic:'\u0625\u0633\u0645\u0627\u0639\u064a\u0644 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'True to his promise',minutes:5,color:'#2E7D5B',paragraphs:['Ismail was the first son of Ibrahim, born to Hajar. He grew up in the valley of Makkah, and from him came the Arabs of the Hijaz and, in time, the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa.'],sections:[{heading:'Growing up by the Kaaba',paragraphs:['As a baby, Ismail was left with his mother in the empty valley of Makkah, and Allah gave them the spring of Zamzam. A tribe called Jurhum passed by, saw birds circling over water, and asked Hajar if they could settle there. She agreed, and Ismail grew up among them, learning their Arabic and later marrying among them.'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 3364']},{heading:'Ready to obey',paragraphs:['When his father told him about the dream of sacrifice, the young Ismail did not run or argue. He said, "My father, do what you are commanded. You will find me, if Allah wills, among the patient." Because of their obedience, Allah ransomed him with a great sacrifice and praised them both.'],refs:['Quran 37:101-107']},{heading:'Builder of the Kaaba',paragraphs:['When Ismail was a grown man, he and his father raised the walls of the Kaaba together. Ismail carried the stones while Ibrahim laid them, and they both prayed that Allah would accept it and make them and their children submit to Him.'],refs:['Quran 2:127-129']},{heading:'How Allah describes him',paragraphs:['Allah says: "And mention in the Book, Ismail. He was true to his promise, and he was a messenger and a prophet. He used to command his family to pray and to give charity, and his Lord was pleased with him."','Allah also mentions him among the patient, and among the best of people.'],refs:['Quran 19:54-55','Quran 21:85','Quran 38:48']}],lesson:'Keep your promises and help your family keep their prayers. Patience and obedience are what made Ismail so loved by Allah.',question:'What did Ismail say when his father told him about the dream?',refs:['Quran 2:127-129','Quran 19:54-55','Quran 37:101-107']},{id:'ishaq',name:'Prophet Ishaq',arabic:'\u0625\u0633\u062d\u0627\u0642 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The son of good news',minutes:3,color:'#B8576B',paragraphs:['Ishaq was the second son of Ibrahim, born to his wife Sarah when both of them were very old. Through Ishaq came Yaqub and the many prophets of the Children of Israel.'],sections:[{heading:'Guests with good news',paragraphs:['Angels came to Ibrahim looking like men. He welcomed them warmly and quickly brought them a roasted calf, but they did not eat, and he felt uneasy. They said, "Do not be afraid. We have been sent to the people of Lut." Then they gave him the good news of a wise son.','Sarah was standing nearby and laughed in surprise. She said, "How can I have a child when I am an old woman and my husband is an old man? That would be amazing!" The angels said, "Are you amazed at Allah\'s command? The mercy and blessings of Allah are upon you, people of this house."'],refs:['Quran 11:69-73','Quran 51:24-30']},{heading:'A prophet among the righteous',paragraphs:['Allah blessed both Ibrahim and Ishaq, and made Ishaq a prophet among the righteous. Allah also gave Ibrahim the good news of Yaqub, the grandson who would come after Ishaq. Allah says He made them leaders who guided by His command, and inspired them to do good, establish prayer and give charity, and they worshipped Him alone.'],refs:['Quran 37:112-113','Quran 21:72-73','Quran 11:71']}],lesson:'Nothing is too hard for Allah. Never give up hope in His mercy, even when something seems impossible.',question:'Why did Sarah laugh when the angels gave her the news?',refs:['Quran 11:69-73','Quran 37:112-113','Quran 51:24-30']}]},1368,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"PROPHETS_B",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'yaqub',name:'Prophet Yaqub',arabic:'\u064a\u0639\u0642\u0648\u0628 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Beautiful patience',minutes:5,color:'#5B2A4A',paragraphs:['Yaqub, also called Israel, was the son of Ishaq and the grandson of Ibrahim. His twelve sons became the twelve tribes of the Children of Israel. His life is a lesson in patience through a grief that lasted many years.'],sections:[{heading:'A father who saw what others did not',paragraphs:['Yaqub loved all his sons, but he saw something special in young Yusuf. When Yusuf told him about a dream of eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowing to him, Yaqub understood that Allah had chosen him. He told Yusuf not to tell his brothers, because Shaytan could stir jealousy between them.','When the brothers came back one night crying, with Yusuf\'s shirt stained with false blood, saying a wolf had eaten him, Yaqub knew they were not telling the truth. He said, "No, your souls have tempted you into something. So beautiful patience. Allah is the One whose help is sought against what you describe."'],refs:['Quran 12:4-6','Quran 12:16-18']},{heading:'Complaining only to Allah',paragraphs:['Years passed. Later his sons lost Binyamin too, the youngest. Yaqub turned away from them, saying, "How great is my sorrow for Yusuf!" His eyes turned white from crying, yet he held his grief inside.','When they told him he would destroy himself with grief, he answered with words every Muslim should remember: "I only complain of my sorrow and grief to Allah, and I know from Allah what you do not know." Then he told them, "Go and search for Yusuf and his brother, and do not lose hope in the mercy of Allah. Only those who reject faith lose hope in Allah\'s mercy."'],refs:['Quran 12:83-87']},{heading:'The shirt and the reunion',paragraphs:['Yusuf, now a leader in Egypt, sent his shirt back with his brothers: "Put it over my father\'s face and he will see again." As soon as the caravan left Egypt, Yaqub said, "I can smell Yusuf." When the shirt was placed on his face, his sight came back. He said, "Did I not tell you that I know from Allah what you do not know?"','His sons begged him to ask Allah to forgive them, and he promised he would. The whole family travelled to Egypt and was reunited after all those years.'],refs:['Quran 12:93-98']},{heading:'His last advice',paragraphs:['When death came to Yaqub, he gathered his sons and asked, "What will you worship after me?" They said, "We will worship your God, the God of your fathers Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, one God, and to Him we submit." This was the same advice Ibrahim had given his own sons: "Allah has chosen this religion for you, so do not die except as Muslims."'],refs:['Quran 2:132-133']}],lesson:'Real patience is not hiding pain; it is taking your pain to Allah and never losing hope in His mercy.',question:'Who did Yaqub say he complained to about his sadness?',refs:['Quran 12','Quran 2:132-133','Quran 19:49']},{id:'yusuf',name:'Prophet Yusuf',arabic:'\u064a\u0648\u0633\u0641 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'From the well to the palace',minutes:14,color:'#7A3E6E',paragraphs:['Allah calls this "the best of stories". It is the only prophet\'s story told from start to finish in one surah, Surah Yusuf. Yusuf was the son of Yaqub, the grandson of Ishaq and the great-grandson of Ibrahim.'],sections:[{heading:'The dream',paragraphs:['One night, young Yusuf dreamed that eleven stars, the sun and the moon were bowing down to him. He told his father, Yaqub, who understood that Allah had chosen Yusuf and would teach him the meaning of dreams. Yaqub warned him, "Do not tell your brothers about your dream, or they will plot against you."'],refs:['Quran 12:4-6']},{heading:'Thrown into the well',paragraphs:['Yusuf\'s older brothers were jealous of how much their father loved him and his younger brother Binyamin. One said, "Kill Yusuf or throw him into some far land, then our father\'s attention will be ours." Another said, "Do not kill him. Throw him into the bottom of a well; some travellers will pick him up."','They persuaded their father to let Yusuf come out with them to play, then threw him into a deep well. Even there, Allah inspired Yusuf that one day he would tell them about what they had done when they would not recognise him. At night they came home crying, with his shirt stained with fake blood, and said a wolf had eaten him.'],refs:['Quran 12:8-18']},{heading:'Sold in Egypt',paragraphs:['A caravan stopped at the well. When they lowered a bucket, Yusuf came up with it. They hid him as goods and sold him in Egypt for a few coins. The man who bought him was a high official, the Aziz, who told his wife, "Look after him well. He may be useful to us, or we may adopt him as a son." Allah settled Yusuf in that land and gave him wisdom and knowledge.'],refs:['Quran 12:19-22']},{heading:'The test in the house',paragraphs:['Yusuf grew into a very handsome young man. The wife of the Aziz tried to tempt him, closed the doors and called him to her. Yusuf said, "I seek refuge in Allah! Your husband is my master and has treated me well. Wrongdoers never succeed." He ran to the door; she tore the back of his shirt as she pulled him, and they found her husband at the door.','She accused Yusuf, but a witness from her own family said, "If his shirt is torn from the front, she is telling the truth. If it is torn from the back, he is telling the truth." It was torn from the back. The women of the city began gossiping about her, so she invited them, gave each a knife and some fruit, and told Yusuf to come out. When they saw him they were so amazed that they cut their hands and said, "This is no human, this is a noble angel!"','Yusuf prayed, "My Lord, prison is dearer to me than what they invite me to." Allah answered his prayer, and even though they knew he was innocent, they put him in prison.'],refs:['Quran 12:23-35']},{heading:'Dreams in prison',paragraphs:['Two young men entered prison with him. One dreamed he was pressing grapes for wine; the other dreamed he carried bread on his head while birds ate from it. Before explaining their dreams, Yusuf used the moment to teach them: "My fellow prisoners, are many different lords better, or Allah, the One, the Overpowering?"','Then he explained: the first would serve wine to the king again, and the second would be executed. Yusuf asked the one who would be freed to mention him to the king, but Shaytan made him forget, and Yusuf stayed in prison for several more years.'],refs:['Quran 12:36-42']},{heading:'The king\'s dream',paragraphs:['One day the king dreamed of seven fat cows being eaten by seven thin cows, and seven green ears of grain and seven dry ones. No one could explain it. Then the freed prisoner remembered Yusuf.','Yusuf explained that Egypt would have seven years of good harvests, followed by seven years of terrible famine. He gave a plan: farm hard for seven years and store the grain in its ears, eating only a little. When the king sent for him, Yusuf refused to leave prison until his name was cleared. The women admitted he was innocent, and the wife of the Aziz confessed, "I tried to tempt him, and he is truthful."','The king made Yusuf his trusted minister. Yusuf asked to be put in charge of the storehouses, saying, "I am a knowledgeable guardian." He prepared Egypt for the famine.'],refs:['Quran 12:43-57']},{heading:'His brothers come',paragraphs:['The famine reached the land of Canaan, and Yusuf\'s brothers came to Egypt to buy food. Yusuf recognised them, but they did not recognise him. He gave them grain and told them to bring their youngest brother, Binyamin, next time. He also secretly put their money back in their bags.','When they returned with Binyamin, Yusuf quietly told Binyamin who he was. Then he had the king\'s cup placed in Binyamin\'s bag so he could keep him in Egypt. The brothers were in despair; they had promised their father to bring Binyamin home.'],refs:['Quran 12:58-82']},{heading:'"No blame on you today"',paragraphs:['On their third visit, the brothers came humbled and poor. Yusuf asked them, "Do you know what you did to Yusuf and his brother when you were ignorant?" They said, "Are you really Yusuf?" He said, "I am Yusuf, and this is my brother. Allah has been gracious to us. Whoever is mindful of Allah and patient, Allah never lets the reward of good people be lost."','They admitted their wrong. Yusuf answered with mercy: "No blame on you today. May Allah forgive you; He is the Most Merciful of the merciful." Centuries later, when the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa conquered Makkah and stood before the people who had persecuted him, he forgave them with the same words.'],refs:['Quran 12:88-92']},{heading:'The dream comes true',paragraphs:['Yusuf sent his shirt to his father, whose sight returned. The whole family came to Egypt. Yusuf raised his parents onto the throne, and his parents and eleven brothers bowed down to him in respect. He said, "My father, this is the meaning of my dream from long ago. My Lord has made it come true."','At the height of his success, Yusuf did not ask for more power. He prayed: "My Lord, You have given me authority and taught me the meaning of events. Creator of the heavens and the earth, You are my protector in this world and the next. Let me die as a Muslim and join me with the righteous."'],refs:['Quran 12:93-101']}],lesson:'Allah\'s plan is bigger than anyone\'s plot. The well, the false accusation and the prison were all steps to the palace. Stay pure when tested, be patient, and forgive when you have the power to take revenge.',question:'What did Yusuf say to his brothers when he finally told them who he was?',refs:['Quran 12:1-111']},{id:'ayyub',name:'Prophet Ayyub',arabic:'\u0623\u064a\u0648\u0628 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The most patient',minutes:4,color:'#5B6B2E',paragraphs:['Ayyub was a prophet whom Allah had blessed with wealth, land, animals and a large family. Then Allah tested him, and his patience became an example for all time.'],sections:[{heading:'Losing everything',paragraphs:['Ayyub lost his wealth and his children, and a long, painful illness came over his body. Many people left him. Through all of it he did not complain about Allah or stop remembering Him. His wife stayed faithfully by his side and cared for him.'],refs:['Quran 21:83','Quran 38:41']},{heading:'A gentle prayer',paragraphs:['After a long time, Ayyub called out to his Lord with one of the most beautiful and polite prayers in the Quran: "Harm has touched me, and You are the Most Merciful of those who show mercy." He did not demand, and he did not even ask directly; he simply told Allah his condition and praised His mercy.'],refs:['Quran 21:83']},{heading:'The cool spring',paragraphs:['Allah answered: "Strike the ground with your foot. Here is a cool spring to wash in and to drink." Ayyub did so, and his illness was taken away. Allah gave him back his family and as many again with them, as a mercy and a reminder for people of understanding.','Ayyub had made an oath during his illness to strike someone a number of times. Allah, out of mercy, told him to take a bundle of thin grass and strike once with it, so he kept his oath without causing harm. Then Allah gave him the highest praise: "We found him patient. What an excellent servant! He always turned back to Allah."'],refs:['Quran 38:41-44','Quran 21:84']}],lesson:'When hardship comes, keep remembering Allah, speak to Him politely about your pain, and trust that relief will come.',question:'What did Allah tell Ayyub to do to find the cool spring?',refs:['Quran 21:83-84','Quran 38:41-44']},{id:'shuayb',name:'Prophet Shuayb',arabic:'\u0634\u0639\u064a\u0628 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Honest weights and measures',minutes:5,color:'#A8573A',paragraphs:['Shuayb was sent to the people of Madyan, a trading town in the north-west of Arabia, and to the people of the Aykah, the thick woods nearby. He was such a powerful speaker that he is sometimes called "the speaker of the prophets".'],sections:[{heading:'Cheating in the market',paragraphs:['The people of Madyan worshipped others besides Allah, and they cheated in business. When they bought, they demanded full measure; when they sold, they gave short weight. They also sat by the roads, threatening travellers and blocking people from the path of Allah.','Shuayb said, "My people, worship Allah. Give full measure and weight with fairness, do not cheat people of their things, and do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set right. What Allah leaves you, even if it is less, is better for you."'],refs:['Quran 7:85-86','Quran 11:84-86','Quran 26:176-183']},{heading:'"Does your prayer tell you this?"',paragraphs:['They mocked him: "Shuayb, does your prayer command you that we should leave what our fathers worshipped, or stop doing what we like with our own money?" They did not want religion to have anything to do with their business.','Shuayb answered with humility: "I do not want to do the very thing I am forbidding you from. I only want to set things right as much as I can. My success is only from Allah. In Him I trust and to Him I turn." He also reminded them of what happened to the people of Nuh, Hud, Salih and Lut.'],refs:['Quran 11:87-90']},{heading:'The end of Madyan',paragraphs:['The leaders threatened to drive Shuayb and the believers out of the town unless they returned to their old ways. Shuayb refused. Then a terrible blast and earthquake seized them, and they lay dead in their homes as if they had never lived there. The people of the Aykah were punished on "the day of the shadow", a dark cloud that brought their end.','Shuayb turned away from the ruins and said, "My people, I delivered my Lord\'s messages and gave you sincere advice. How can I grieve for people who rejected the truth?"'],refs:['Quran 7:88-93','Quran 11:94-95','Quran 26:189']}],lesson:'Islam is not only prayer; it is also honesty in business. A Muslim never cheats in buying, selling or work, even a little.',question:'How did the people of Madyan cheat others?',refs:['Quran 7:85-93','Quran 11:84-95','Quran 26:176-191']},{id:'musa',name:'Prophet Musa',arabic:'\u0645\u0648\u0633\u0649 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The one Allah spoke to',minutes:18,color:'#2E4A7A',paragraphs:['Musa is mentioned in the Quran more than any other prophet. He is called Kalimullah, the one to whom Allah spoke directly. His story moves from a baby in a basket to a leader who stood before the most powerful tyrant on earth.'],sections:[{heading:'A baby on the river',paragraphs:['Pharaoh ruled Egypt with cruelty. He divided people into groups and oppressed the Children of Israel, killing their baby boys. When Musa was born, Allah inspired his mother: "Nurse him, and when you fear for him, put him in the river and do not fear or grieve. We will return him to you and make him a messenger."','She placed him in a basket on the Nile, and the water carried him straight to Pharaoh\'s palace. Pharaoh\'s wife, Asiyah, loved him at first sight and said, "A joy for me and for you. Do not kill him." Baby Musa refused to drink from any nurse. His sister, who had been following the basket, said, "Shall I show you a family who will look after him?" And so Musa was returned to his own mother, exactly as Allah had promised.'],refs:['Quran 28:3-13','Quran 20:38-40']},{heading:'Leaving Egypt',paragraphs:['Musa grew up strong and wise in the palace. One day he saw a man from Pharaoh\'s people fighting a man from the Children of Israel. Musa struck the Egyptian to stop him, and the man died. Musa had not meant to kill him. He immediately prayed, "My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me," and Allah forgave him.','When he learned that Pharaoh\'s chiefs were planning to kill him, Musa fled Egypt, alone and afraid, praying, "My Lord, save me from the wrongdoing people."'],refs:['Quran 28:14-21']},{heading:'Kindness at the well of Madyan',paragraphs:['Musa reached the wells of Madyan, tired and hungry. He saw shepherds crowding to water their flocks, and two young women holding their sheep back, waiting. They told him they could not water until the shepherds left, and their father was a very old man. Musa watered their flock for them, then sat in the shade and prayed: "My Lord, I am in need of whatever good You send down to me."','Soon one of the women came back, walking shyly, and said her father wanted to reward him. The old man welcomed Musa, and one daughter said, "Father, hire him. The best person to hire is the strong and trustworthy." Musa married one of the daughters and worked for her father for ten years. Many scholars say this old man was Prophet Shuayb; others say he was another righteous man.'],refs:['Quran 28:22-28']},{heading:'The fire on the mountain',paragraphs:['On the way back to Egypt with his family, on a cold dark night, Musa saw a fire on the side of Mount Tur. He went to it hoping for a burning stick or for directions. A voice called: "O Musa, I am your Lord. Take off your sandals; you are in the sacred valley of Tuwa. I have chosen you, so listen to what is revealed. I am Allah; there is no god but Me, so worship Me and establish prayer to remember Me."','Allah gave him two signs. When he threw down his staff, it became a moving snake; when he picked it up, it became a staff again. When he put his hand under his arm, it came out shining white without any illness. Then Allah said, "Go to Pharaoh; he has gone beyond all limits."','Musa asked for help: "My Lord, open my chest for me, make my task easy, untie the knot from my tongue so they understand my speech, and give me a helper from my family, my brother Harun." Allah said, "You have been granted what you asked, Musa."'],refs:['Quran 20:9-36','Quran 28:29-35']},{heading:'Standing before Pharaoh',paragraphs:['Allah told Musa and Harun to speak to Pharaoh gently, "so that he may remember or fear". They said, "We are messengers of your Lord. Let the Children of Israel go with us." Pharaoh asked, "Who is your Lord, Musa?" Musa answered, "Our Lord is the One who gave everything its form and then guided it."','Pharaoh reminded Musa that he had grown up in his palace and accused him of being ungrateful. He called himself "your lord, the most high" and threatened to imprison Musa. Musa showed him the staff and the shining hand. Pharaoh called it magic and set a day for a contest with his best magicians.'],refs:['Quran 20:42-59','Quran 26:16-37','Quran 79:15-24']},{heading:'The magicians',paragraphs:['On the festival day, in front of a huge crowd, the magicians threw down their ropes and sticks, and by their magic they seemed to move like snakes. Musa felt a moment of fear. Allah said, "Do not fear. You will be the one on top. Throw what is in your right hand." Musa threw his staff, and it swallowed everything they had made.','The magicians knew this was no magic. They fell down in prostration: "We believe in the Lord of Harun and Musa!" Pharaoh threatened to cut off their hands and feet and crucify them. They answered, "We will never prefer you over the clear signs that came to us. Decide whatever you will; you can only decide about this worldly life." In the morning they were magicians; by evening they were martyrs.'],refs:['Quran 20:60-73','Quran 7:113-126']},{heading:'The signs and the sea',paragraphs:['Pharaoh still refused. Allah sent sign after sign on Egypt: years of drought and poor harvests, then floods, locusts, lice, frogs and blood. Each time, they begged Musa to pray for relief and promised to let the Children of Israel go, and each time they broke their promise.','Then Allah told Musa to leave at night with the Children of Israel. Pharaoh chased them with his army and caught up at sunrise by the sea. The people cried, "We are surely caught!" Musa said, "No! My Lord is with me; He will guide me." Allah told him to strike the sea with his staff. It split into two, each side like a great mountain, with a dry path between. The believers crossed safely.','Pharaoh followed them into the sea, and the waters closed over him and his army. As he was drowning he cried, "I believe that there is no god but the One the Children of Israel believe in." But it was too late. Allah said, "Today We will save your body, so that you may be a sign for those after you."'],refs:['Quran 7:130-136','Quran 26:52-68','Quran 10:88-92','Quran 20:77-79']},{heading:'In the desert',paragraphs:['In the desert, Allah shaded the Children of Israel with clouds and sent them manna and quails to eat. When they were thirsty, Musa struck a rock with his staff and twelve springs gushed out, one for each tribe.','Musa went to the mountain for forty nights to receive the Torah, leaving Harun in charge. While he was away, a man called the Samiri melted the people\'s gold jewellery and made a golden calf that made a lowing sound, and many began to worship it. Musa returned furious and sad. He burned the calf, scattered it in the sea, and the people repented. Allah gave Musa the Tablets with guidance and light.','Later, when Allah commanded them to enter the holy land, they refused out of fear and said, "Go, you and your Lord, and fight. We will sit here." So Allah made them wander in the desert for forty years.'],refs:['Quran 2:57-60','Quran 7:142-154','Quran 20:83-98','Quran 5:20-26']},{heading:'Musa and al-Khidr',paragraphs:['Once, Musa was asked who was the most knowledgeable person on earth, and he said, "I am." Allah told him that there was a servant of His, at the meeting of the two seas, who had knowledge Musa did not have. Musa set out to find him: al-Khidr.','Musa asked to follow him and learn. Al-Khidr said, "You will not be able to be patient with me." On their journey al-Khidr made a hole in a boat that had carried them for free, killed a young boy, and rebuilt a falling wall in a town that refused them food. Each time Musa could not stay silent.','Then al-Khidr explained: the boat belonged to poor people, and a king ahead was seizing every good boat; the damage saved it. The boy would have grown up to lead his believing parents into disbelief, and Allah would give them a better child. The wall hid treasure belonging to two orphans whose father had been righteous. "I did not do it of my own accord," he said. Musa learned that Allah\'s wisdom is often hidden behind things that look bad.'],refs:['Quran 18:60-82','Sahih al-Bukhari 122']}],lesson:'No tyrant is too strong for Allah. Ask Allah for help with your weaknesses as Musa did, be kind even when you are struggling yourself, and trust that Allah\'s plan has wisdom even when you cannot see it.',question:'What did Musa say when the people thought they were trapped by the sea?',refs:['Quran 20:9-98','Quran 26:10-68','Quran 28:3-35','Quran 7:103-155','Quran 18:60-82']},{id:'harun',name:'Prophet Harun',arabic:'\u0647\u0627\u0631\u0648\u0646 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The brother who helped',minutes:4,color:'#4E5A8C',paragraphs:['Harun was the older brother of Musa. He was given prophethood as an answer to Musa\'s prayer, a gift from Allah to help his brother.'],sections:[{heading:'An answered prayer',paragraphs:['When Allah sent Musa to Pharaoh, Musa asked, "Give me a helper from my family, Harun my brother. Strengthen me through him and let him share my task." He said, "My brother Harun is more eloquent than me, so send him with me to support me." Allah granted it. The Quran says: "We gave him, out of Our mercy, his brother Harun as a prophet."','Together they stood before Pharaoh, delivering the message side by side.'],refs:['Quran 20:29-36','Quran 28:34-35','Quran 19:53']},{heading:'Left in charge',paragraphs:['When Musa went to the mountain for forty nights, he told Harun, "Take my place among my people, set things right, and do not follow the way of those who cause corruption."','While Musa was away, the Samiri made the golden calf. Harun warned the people, "My people, you are being tested by this. Your Lord is the Most Merciful, so follow me and obey my command." Most would not listen.'],refs:['Quran 7:142','Quran 20:90-91']},{heading:'"O son of my mother"',paragraphs:['When Musa returned and saw the calf, he grabbed his brother by the beard and head in anger. Harun said gently, "O son of my mother, do not seize me by my beard or my head. I was afraid you would say that I divided the Children of Israel. The people overpowered me and almost killed me."','Musa\'s anger calmed and he prayed, "My Lord, forgive me and my brother, and admit us into Your mercy." In the hadith of the night journey, the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa met Harun in the fifth heaven.'],refs:['Quran 7:150-151','Quran 20:92-94','Sahih al-Bukhari 3207']}],lesson:'Good brothers and sisters support each other. Answer anger with gentle words, and stay firm on the truth even when most people go the wrong way.',question:'Why did Musa ask Allah to send Harun with him?',refs:['Quran 20:29-36','Quran 20:90-94','Quran 28:34-35']},{id:'dhulkifl',name:'Prophet Dhul-Kifl',arabic:'\u0630\u0648 \u0627\u0644\u0643\u0641\u0644 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Among the patient',minutes:2,color:'#9E3F5C',paragraphs:['Dhul-Kifl is mentioned in the Quran twice, each time beside other great prophets. Very little is told about his life, and that itself is a lesson: Allah\'s praise is enough.'],sections:[{heading:'What the Quran says',paragraphs:['Allah says: "And Ismail, Idris and Dhul-Kifl, all were among the patient. We admitted them into Our mercy; they were among the righteous." And: "Remember Ismail, al-Yasa and Dhul-Kifl, all among the best."','His name means "the one with a portion" or "the one who took on a responsibility". Many scholars explain that he promised to take on a heavy responsibility, such as judging fairly, praying through the night and fasting by day without getting angry, and he kept that promise. We do not know the details for certain, so we hold on to what Allah tells us: he was patient, righteous and among the best.'],refs:['Quran 21:85-86','Quran 38:48']}],lesson:'Keep your commitments and be patient, even when no one is watching. Allah sees and remembers.',question:'Which two qualities does the Quran give Dhul-Kifl?',refs:['Quran 21:85-86','Quran 38:48']},{id:'dawud',name:'Prophet Dawud',arabic:'\u062f\u0627\u0648\u062f \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The king who glorified Allah',minutes:7,color:'#C9703A',paragraphs:['Dawud was a young soldier who became a prophet and a king. Allah gave him the Zabur (the Psalms), a beautiful voice, wisdom in judging, and great skill with his hands.'],sections:[{heading:'The young man who faced Jalut',paragraphs:['Long after Musa, the Children of Israel asked their prophet for a king to lead them in battle. Allah chose Talut. On the way to fight, Talut tested the army at a river: whoever drank from it more than a handful would not come with him. Most of them drank, and only a small, faithful group crossed.','When they saw the huge army of Jalut (Goliath), some said, "We have no power today against Jalut and his soldiers." But the believers said, "How many times has a small group defeated a large group by Allah\'s permission? Allah is with the patient." They prayed, "Our Lord, pour patience upon us, make our feet firm and give us victory." In the battle, young Dawud killed Jalut, and Allah gave him kingship and wisdom.'],refs:['Quran 2:246-251']},{heading:'The mountains and the birds',paragraphs:['Allah gave Dawud a voice so beautiful that when he glorified Allah, the mountains and the birds joined in with him, morning and evening. Allah gave him the Zabur. The Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa once heard a companion reciting beautifully and said he had been given "one of the flutes of the family of Dawud".','Allah made iron soft in Dawud\'s hands, and taught him to make chain armour, measuring the links carefully, to protect people in battle.'],refs:['Quran 34:10-11','Quran 38:17-19','Quran 21:79-80','Quran 17:55','Sahih al-Bukhari 5048']},{heading:'A fair judge',paragraphs:['Allah made Dawud a khalifah on earth and told him, "Judge between people with truth, and do not follow your desires." Once two men climbed over the wall of his prayer room and startled him. One said his brother had ninety-nine sheep and still demanded his one sheep. Dawud quickly said the brother had wronged him. Then he realised it was a test for him to judge carefully, and he asked Allah\'s forgiveness and bowed down in repentance.','In another case, sheep had wandered at night into someone\'s field and eaten the crops. Allah gave young Sulayman, Dawud\'s son, special understanding of the fairest solution, and Dawud accepted his son\'s judgement.'],refs:['Quran 38:21-26','Quran 21:78-79']},{heading:'The best fasting and prayer',paragraphs:['Even as a king, Dawud ate only from what he earned with his own hands. The Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa said that no one eats better food than what they earn with their own hands, and that Dawud ate from his own work.','The Prophet \ufdfa also said that the most beloved fasting to Allah is the fasting of Dawud, who fasted one day and broke his fast the next, and the most beloved night prayer is Dawud\'s: he slept half the night, prayed a third, and slept a sixth.'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 2072','Sahih al-Bukhari 1131']}],lesson:'Courage comes from trusting Allah, not from size. Even with power and wealth, Dawud worked with his own hands, judged fairly and spent his nights in worship.',question:'What did the faithful soldiers say when they saw Jalut\'s huge army?',refs:['Quran 2:246-251','Quran 34:10-11','Quran 38:17-26','Quran 21:78-80']}]},1369,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"PROPHETS_C",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return h}});const h=[{id:'sulayman',name:'Prophet Sulayman',arabic:'\u0633\u0644\u064a\u0645\u0627\u0646 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The king who understood the ants',minutes:9,color:'#7A3E6E',paragraphs:['Sulayman was the son of Dawud. Allah gave him a kingdom like no one before or after him: the wind obeyed him, jinn worked for him, and he understood the speech of birds and animals. Yet he always remembered that all of it was a test from Allah.'],sections:[{heading:'A gift with a prayer',paragraphs:['Sulayman inherited prophethood and knowledge from Dawud. He said, "People, we have been taught the speech of birds and given a share of everything. This is clear favour."','He prayed: "My Lord, forgive me and give me a kingdom that no one after me will have. You are the Giver." Allah made the wind run at his command, carrying him a month\'s journey in a morning and a month\'s journey in an evening. Jinn built palaces, statues, great basins and huge cooking pots for him, and Allah made a spring of molten copper flow for him.'],refs:['Quran 27:16','Quran 38:35-38','Quran 34:12-13','Quran 21:81-82']},{heading:'The valley of the ants',paragraphs:['Once Sulayman marched with his huge army of men, jinn and birds. As they reached a valley of ants, one ant called out, "Ants, go into your homes so that Sulayman and his soldiers do not crush you without noticing."','Sulayman heard her and smiled, laughing at her words. Instead of feeling proud, he prayed: "My Lord, inspire me to be thankful for the blessings You gave me and my parents, and to do good that pleases You, and admit me by Your mercy among Your righteous servants."'],refs:['Quran 27:17-19']},{heading:'The hoopoe\'s news',paragraphs:['One day Sulayman checked on the birds and noticed the hoopoe was missing. When it returned, it said, "I have found out something you do not know. I came from Saba with certain news. I found a woman ruling them, who has been given everything and has a great throne. I found her and her people bowing to the sun instead of Allah."','Sulayman said, "We will see whether you are telling the truth." He sent the hoopoe with a letter: "In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Do not be proud against me, but come to me in submission."'],refs:['Quran 27:20-31']},{heading:'The Queen of Saba',paragraphs:['The Queen, called Bilqis by historians, consulted her advisors and wisely decided to send a gift instead of going to war. Sulayman refused the gift: "Do you try to help me with wealth? What Allah has given me is better than what He gave you."','Before she arrived, Sulayman asked who could bring her throne. One of the jinn said he could bring it before Sulayman stood up from his seat. But a man who had knowledge of the Book said, "I will bring it to you before you blink." In an instant the throne was there. Sulayman said, "This is from the favour of my Lord, to test me whether I am thankful or ungrateful."','When the Queen came, she was asked, "Is your throne like this?" She said, "It is as if it were the same." Then she was invited into a palace whose floor was made of clear glass over water. Thinking it was a pool, she lifted her dress. Sulayman told her it was smooth glass. She understood the greatness of Allah and said, "My Lord, I have wronged myself, and I submit with Sulayman to Allah, the Lord of the worlds."'],refs:['Quran 27:32-44']},{heading:'Leaning on his staff',paragraphs:['Sulayman died standing, leaning on his staff, while the jinn kept working, thinking he was watching them. Nothing showed them he had died until a small creature of the earth ate through his staff and he fell. Then the jinn realised that if they really knew the unseen, they would not have kept working so hard in humiliation.'],refs:['Quran 34:14']}],lesson:'The more Allah gives you, the more thankful and humble you should be. Even a king listened to an ant, and even the jinn cannot know the unseen.',question:'What did Sulayman do when he heard the ant speak?',refs:['Quran 27:15-44','Quran 34:12-14','Quran 38:30-40','Quran 21:78-82']},{id:'ilyas',name:'Prophet Ilyas',arabic:'\u0625\u0644\u064a\u0627\u0633 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Against the idol Baal',minutes:3,color:'#2E7D5B',paragraphs:['Ilyas was sent to the Children of Israel at a time when many of them had started worshipping an idol called Baal.'],sections:[{heading:'His call',paragraphs:['Allah says: "Ilyas was one of the messengers. He said to his people, \'Will you not be mindful of Allah? Do you call on Baal and leave the Best of creators, Allah, your Lord and the Lord of your forefathers?\'"','Most of his people rejected him. Allah saved His sincere servants among them, and left praise for Ilyas among later generations: "Peace be upon Ilyasin. That is how We reward those who do good. He was one of Our believing servants."'],refs:['Quran 37:123-132','Quran 6:85']}],lesson:'Nothing deserves worship except Allah, the Best of creators. Do not swap Him for anything made by people.',question:'What did Ilyas ask his people about Baal?',refs:['Quran 37:123-132','Quran 6:85']},{id:'alyasa',name:'Prophet Al-Yasa',arabic:'\u0627\u0644\u064a\u0633\u0639 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Among the best',minutes:2,color:'#B8576B',paragraphs:['Al-Yasa (Elisha) came after Ilyas and continued calling the Children of Israel to Allah.'],sections:[{heading:'What the Quran says',paragraphs:['Allah names him among the prophets He guided and favoured above the worlds: "And Ismail, al-Yasa, Yunus and Lut, each of them We favoured over the worlds." And again: "Remember Ismail, al-Yasa and Dhul-Kifl, all among the best."','Historians say he was a student of Ilyas and carried on his mission after him. The Quran does not give more details, but being named "among the best" by Allah is a great honour.'],refs:['Quran 6:86','Quran 38:48']}],lesson:'Carry on the good work of those who taught you. Allah values faithfulness, not fame.',question:'How does Allah describe al-Yasa in Surah Sad?',refs:['Quran 6:86','Quran 38:48']},{id:'yunus',name:'Prophet Yunus',arabic:'\u064a\u0648\u0646\u0633 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Inside the whale',minutes:6,color:'#1F6E8C',paragraphs:['Yunus, also called Dhun-Nun ("the man of the fish"), was sent to the people of Nineveh, a large city of over a hundred thousand people.'],sections:[{heading:'Leaving in anger',paragraphs:['Yunus called his people to Allah for a long time, but they kept rejecting him. Upset with them, he left the city before Allah gave him permission, thinking Allah would not put him in difficulty for it.','He boarded a ship that was full of people and cargo. A storm hit, and the ship was too heavy. The sailors drew lots to decide who would be thrown overboard to lighten the load. The lot fell on Yunus three times, so he was thrown into the sea.'],refs:['Quran 21:87','Quran 37:139-141']},{heading:'In the darkness',paragraphs:['A huge fish swallowed Yunus. He found himself in layers of darkness: the darkness of the night, the darkness of the sea, and the darkness inside the fish. There, he called out to Allah with words that have become one of the most powerful duas: "La ilaha illa anta, subhanaka, inni kuntu minaz-zalimin", meaning "There is no god but You. Glory be to You. I have been among the wrongdoers."','Allah says that if Yunus had not been one of those who glorify Him, he would have stayed in the fish\'s belly until the Day of Resurrection. Allah answered him and saved him from his distress, and said, "That is how We save the believers." The Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa said that no Muslim calls on Allah with this dua for anything, except that Allah answers him.'],refs:['Quran 21:87-88','Quran 37:142-144','Sunan at-Tirmidhi 3505']},{heading:'The plant on the shore',paragraphs:['The fish threw Yunus out onto an empty shore. He was weak and sick, and Allah made a gourd plant grow over him to shade and nourish him while he recovered.'],refs:['Quran 37:145-146']},{heading:'A whole city believes',paragraphs:['Meanwhile, his people saw signs of the coming punishment and all turned to Allah together, crying and repenting. Allah accepted them and removed the punishment. The Quran says they are the only town that believed in time and benefited from its belief.','Allah sent Yunus back to them, a hundred thousand or more, and they all believed, and Allah let them enjoy life for a time.'],refs:['Quran 10:98','Quran 37:147-148']}],lesson:'No darkness is too deep for Allah to hear you. Admit your mistakes, glorify Allah, and never give up on your people or on Allah\'s mercy.',question:'What dua did Yunus say inside the fish?',refs:['Quran 21:87-88','Quran 37:139-148','Quran 10:98','Quran 68:48-50']},{id:'zakariyya',name:'Prophet Zakariyya',arabic:'\u0632\u0643\u0631\u064a\u0627 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'A quiet prayer answered',minutes:5,color:'#5B2A4A',paragraphs:['Zakariyya was a prophet who served in the temple in Jerusalem. He was the guardian of a young girl named Maryam, who would become the mother of Isa.'],sections:[{heading:'Food out of season',paragraphs:['Maryam\'s mother had promised her child to the service of Allah before she was born. Zakariyya became Maryam\'s guardian. Whenever he went into her prayer room, he found fresh fruit with her, summer fruit in winter and winter fruit in summer. He asked, "Maryam, where does this come from?" She said, "It is from Allah. Allah gives to whoever He wills without limit."'],refs:['Quran 3:35-37']},{heading:'A secret call',paragraphs:['Seeing this, Zakariyya was moved to ask Allah for something that seemed impossible. He was very old and his wife had never been able to have children. He called to his Lord in a quiet, private prayer: "My Lord, my bones have grown weak and my head has turned white with grey, and I have never been disappointed in calling on You. Give me from Yourself an heir who will inherit from me and from the family of Yaqub, and make him pleasing to You." He also prayed: "My Lord, do not leave me alone, and You are the best of inheritors."'],refs:['Quran 3:38','Quran 19:2-6','Quran 21:89']},{heading:'The good news of Yahya',paragraphs:['While he stood praying in the temple, the angels called to him: "Allah gives you good news of a son named Yahya. No one has been given that name before." Zakariyya asked, amazed, "My Lord, how can I have a son when my wife is barren and I am so old?" Allah said, "It is easy for Me. I created you before, when you were nothing."','He asked for a sign. Allah said, "Your sign is that you will not speak to people for three nights, though you are well, except by gestures." So he came out to his people and signalled to them to glorify Allah morning and evening.','Allah says: "We answered him and gave him Yahya, and made his wife able to have a child. They used to hurry to do good deeds, called on Us with hope and fear, and were humble before Us."'],refs:['Quran 3:39-41','Quran 19:7-11','Quran 21:90']}],lesson:'Never think something is too big to ask Allah for. Make dua quietly and sincerely, and keep doing good while you wait.',question:'What did Zakariyya find with Maryam that surprised him?',refs:['Quran 3:35-41','Quran 19:1-15','Quran 21:89-90']},{id:'yahya',name:'Prophet Yahya',arabic:'\u064a\u062d\u064a\u0649 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'Wise as a child',minutes:3,color:'#A8573A',paragraphs:['Yahya (John) was the miracle son of Zakariyya, born to elderly parents. He was a cousin of Isa and announced his coming.'],sections:[{heading:'Given wisdom young',paragraphs:['Allah told him: "O Yahya, hold firmly to the Book." Allah gave him wisdom while he was still a child, along with tenderness from Allah and purity. He was mindful of Allah, kind to his parents, and never arrogant or disobedient.','Allah says: "Peace be upon him the day he was born, the day he dies, and the day he is raised alive." He was described as noble, chaste, a prophet among the righteous, and one who would confirm a word from Allah, meaning Isa.'],refs:['Quran 19:12-15','Quran 3:39','Quran 6:85']},{heading:'Met in the second heaven',paragraphs:['On the night journey, the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa met Yahya and Isa together in the second heaven. They welcomed him as a righteous brother and a righteous prophet.'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 3207']}],lesson:'You are never too young to be wise, kind to your parents and close to Allah.',question:'What three things did Allah say about Yahya\'s character towards his parents and people?',refs:['Quran 19:12-15','Quran 3:39']},{id:'isa',name:'Prophet Isa',arabic:'\u0639\u064a\u0633\u0649 \u0639\u0644\u064a\u0647 \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645',title:'The miracle of Maryam\'s son',minutes:12,color:'#8C2F4A',paragraphs:['Isa (Jesus) is one of the five greatest messengers. Muslims love and honour him and his mother Maryam, the only woman named in the Quran, with a whole surah in her name.'],sections:[{heading:'Maryam, chosen above women',paragraphs:['Maryam grew up in the temple under the care of Zakariyya, devoted to worship. The angels said to her: "Maryam, Allah has chosen you and purified you, and chosen you above the women of the worlds. Be devoted to your Lord, prostrate and bow with those who bow."'],refs:['Quran 3:42-43','Quran 3:35-37']},{heading:'The angel\'s visit',paragraphs:['One day, when Maryam was alone, the angel Jibril came to her in the form of a man. She was frightened and said, "I seek refuge in the Most Merciful from you, if you fear Allah." He said, "I am only a messenger from your Lord, to give you a pure son." She asked, "How can I have a son when no man has touched me?" He said, "Your Lord says: it is easy for Me, and We will make him a sign for people and a mercy from Us."'],refs:['Quran 19:16-21','Quran 3:45-47']},{heading:'Under the palm tree',paragraphs:['Maryam went far away. When the pains of birth came, she held on to the trunk of a palm tree and cried, "I wish I had died before this and been forgotten." A voice called to her from below: "Do not grieve. Your Lord has placed a stream beneath you. Shake the trunk of the palm tree towards you and fresh ripe dates will fall. Eat, drink and be at peace. If you see anyone, say, \'I have vowed a fast of silence to the Most Merciful, so I will not speak to anyone today.\'"'],refs:['Quran 19:22-26']},{heading:'The baby speaks',paragraphs:['Maryam carried the baby back to her people. They were shocked: "Maryam, you have done something terrible! Your father was not a bad man and your mother was not unchaste." She only pointed to the baby. They said, "How can we speak to a baby in the cradle?"','Then baby Isa spoke: "I am the servant of Allah. He has given me the Book and made me a prophet. He has made me blessed wherever I am, and commanded me to pray and give charity as long as I live, and to be kind to my mother, and He has not made me arrogant or miserable. Peace be upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I am raised alive."'],refs:['Quran 19:27-33']},{heading:'Miracles by Allah\'s permission',paragraphs:['Allah taught Isa the Torah and gave him the Injil (Gospel), and sent him to the Children of Israel. He said, "I have come with a sign from your Lord. I shape for you from clay the form of a bird, then breathe into it, and it becomes a bird by Allah\'s permission. I heal the blind and the leper, and bring the dead to life by Allah\'s permission. I tell you what you eat and what you store in your homes."','Every miracle was "by Allah\'s permission". Isa never claimed power of his own. He always said, "Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him. This is a straight path."'],refs:['Quran 3:48-51','Quran 5:110']},{heading:'The disciples and the table',paragraphs:['A small group of faithful disciples, the Hawariyyun, believed in him. They said, "We are Allah\'s helpers. We believe in Allah; bear witness that we are Muslims." Once they asked Isa whether his Lord could send down a table spread with food from the sky, so their hearts would be at rest. Isa prayed for it, and Allah sent it down as a sign, with a warning that whoever disbelieved after it would face a severe punishment. The fifth surah, al-Maidah ("The Table"), is named after this.','Isa also gave good news of a messenger who would come after him, whose name would be Ahmad, another name of the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa.'],refs:['Quran 3:52-53','Quran 5:111-115','Quran 61:6']},{heading:'Raised up, and his return',paragraphs:['His enemies plotted to kill him, but Allah was the best of planners. The Quran is clear: they did not kill him and did not crucify him; it was only made to appear so to them. Allah raised Isa up to Himself.','The Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa told us that Isa will return before the Day of Judgement as a just ruler. He will break the cross, end injustice and defeat the Dajjal (the false messiah), and people of the Book will believe in him. On the Day of Judgement, Isa will tell Allah that he only ever told people, "Worship Allah, my Lord and your Lord."'],refs:['Quran 4:157-159','Quran 3:54-55','Quran 5:116-118','Sahih al-Bukhari 3448']}],lesson:'Isa is a noble messenger and a servant of Allah, created by Allah\'s word just as Adam was created without parents. Every miracle comes from Allah alone.',question:'What were the first words baby Isa said about himself?',refs:['Quran 3:42-59','Quran 4:157-159','Quran 5:110-118','Quran 19:16-36','Quran 61:6']},{id:'muhammad',name:'Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa',arabic:'\u0645\u062d\u0645\u062f \ufdfa',title:'The Seal of the Prophets',minutes:16,color:'#2E7D5B',paragraphs:['Muhammad \ufdfa is the last messenger, sent as a mercy to all the worlds. Allah says, "You have in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example." His story is the story of how Islam reached the whole world. (For the full timeline, open the Seerah.)'],sections:[{heading:'An orphan in Makkah',paragraphs:['He was born in Makkah in the Year of the Elephant, the year Abraha marched an army with elephants to destroy the Kaaba and Allah destroyed them with flocks of birds. His father Abdullah died before he was born. As a baby he was nursed in the desert by Halimah of the Banu Sad. His mother Aminah died when he was six, and his grandfather Abdul-Muttalib when he was eight. Then his uncle Abu Talib raised him. Allah reminds him: "Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?"','He grew up so honest that the people of Makkah called him al-Amin, the trustworthy. At 25 he married Khadijah, a respected businesswoman who had seen his honesty when he traded for her. When the tribes argued over who would place the Black Stone back in the Kaaba, he solved it by putting it on a cloth and letting every tribe lift a corner.'],refs:['Quran 105','Quran 93:6-8','Quran 33:21']},{heading:'"Read!"',paragraphs:['At about 40, he often went alone to the cave of Hira on a mountain near Makkah to think and worship. One night in Ramadan, the angel Jibril came and said, "Read!" He said, "I am not a reader." The angel squeezed him and repeated it three times, then recited: "Read in the name of your Lord who created, created man from a clinging clot. Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know."','He rushed home trembling and said, "Cover me, cover me!" Khadijah comforted him: "Allah will never disgrace you. You keep ties with your family, help the weak, honour guests and help people in hardship." She became the first person to believe in him.'],refs:['Quran 96:1-5','Sahih al-Bukhari 3']},{heading:'Calling to Allah',paragraphs:['For three years he called people quietly. Among the first believers were Khadijah, his young cousin Ali, his freed servant Zayd and his close friend Abu Bakr. Then Allah commanded him to call openly. He climbed the hill of Safa and called the tribes of Quraysh to worship Allah alone.','The leaders of Makkah turned against him. The weak believers were tortured; Bilal was laid on burning sand with a rock on his chest and kept saying, "Ahad, Ahad" (One, One). Sumayyah became the first martyr of Islam. Some Muslims migrated to Abyssinia, where a just Christian king protected them. For three years the Muslims were boycotted in a valley until they ate leaves.'],refs:['Quran 26:214','Quran 15:94']},{heading:'The year of sadness and Ta\'if',paragraphs:['In one year he lost both Khadijah and Abu Talib. He went to the town of Ta\'if hoping its people would listen, but they set children and servants on him who threw stones until his feet bled. When the angel of the mountains offered to crush the town, he refused, saying he hoped their children would worship Allah. That hope came true.'],refs:['Sahih al-Bukhari 3231']},{heading:'The night journey',paragraphs:['Then Allah honoured him with the Isra and Miraj. In one night he was taken from Makkah to Masjid al-Aqsa in Jerusalem, where he led all the prophets in prayer, and then up through the seven heavens. He met Adam, Yahya and Isa, Yusuf, Idris, Harun, Musa and Ibrahim, and was given the five daily prayers as a gift for his ummah.'],refs:['Quran 17:1','Quran 53:13-18','Sahih al-Bukhari 3207']},{heading:'The Hijrah to Madinah',paragraphs:['People from Yathrib (later Madinah) accepted Islam and invited him to their city. Quraysh plotted to kill him on one night. He left with Abu Bakr while Ali slept in his bed. They hid in the cave of Thawr for three days; the trackers stood right at its mouth. Abu Bakr whispered that if they looked down at their feet they would see them. The Prophet \ufdfa said, "Do not be sad; Allah is with us."','In Madinah he built the mosque, made the Muhajirun (migrants) and the Ansar (helpers) brothers, and set up a community based on justice. The Islamic calendar begins from this Hijrah.'],refs:['Quran 9:40','Quran 8:30','Quran 59:8-9']},{heading:'Badr, Uhud and the Trench',paragraphs:['At Badr, 313 poorly armed Muslims faced an army three times their size, and Allah gave them victory. At Uhud, some archers left their posts against his orders, and the Muslims suffered losses; the Prophet \ufdfa himself was injured. At the Battle of the Trench, the Muslims dug a long trench around Madinah on Salman al-Farisi\'s advice, and a powerful wind scattered the besieging armies.'],refs:['Quran 3:123','Quran 3:152-153','Quran 33:9-11']},{heading:'Peace, and the return to Makkah',paragraphs:['At Hudaybiyah he agreed a treaty that seemed unfair to some companions, but Allah called it "a clear victory", because in the peace that followed, huge numbers of people accepted Islam. When Quraysh broke the treaty, he marched on Makkah with ten thousand believers and entered it almost without fighting.','He removed the 360 idols from around the Kaaba, reciting, "Truth has come and falsehood has gone." Then he stood before the people who had tortured, boycotted and fought him for twenty years and forgave them, saying the words of Yusuf: "No blame on you today."'],refs:['Quran 48:1','Quran 17:81','Quran 110']},{heading:'The farewell',paragraphs:['In his last Hajj, he spoke to over a hundred thousand people at Arafah. He taught that all people are equal, Arab and non-Arab, black and white, except in taqwa; that their lives, wealth and honour are sacred; to treat women well; and to hold on to the Quran and his Sunnah. Allah revealed: "Today I have perfected your religion for you, completed My favour upon you, and chosen Islam as your religion."','A few months later, at about 63, he fell ill and passed away in Madinah, in the room of his wife Aishah. Abu Bakr told the grieving people: "Whoever worshipped Muhammad, Muhammad has died. Whoever worships Allah, Allah is Ever-Living and never dies." He left no wealth, but he left the Quran, his Sunnah and an ummah that today numbers nearly two billion. Allahumma salli ala Muhammad.'],refs:['Quran 5:3','Quran 3:144','Quran 33:40','Quran 21:107']}],lesson:'He was the best of people in character: honest before prophethood, patient in hardship, merciful in victory. Follow his example and send blessings on him often.',question:'What did the Prophet \ufdfa say to the people of Makkah when he could have taken revenge?',refs:['Quran 33:21','Quran 33:40','Quran 21:107','Quran 96:1-5','Quran 17:1','Quran 9:40','Quran 48:1','Quran 5:3']}]},1370,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"STORIES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'bilal',name:'Bilal ibn Rabah',arabic:'\u0628\u0644\u0627\u0644 \u0628\u0646 \u0631\u0628\u0627\u062d',title:'The First Voice of the Adhan',minutes:3,color:'#0F5C4D',paragraphs:['Long ago in Makkah, there was a man named Bilal. He was enslaved, which means he was not free to live his own life. His master was a cruel man who hated Islam.','When Bilal became Muslim, his master was very angry. He made Bilal lie on the burning hot sand and put a heavy rock on his chest. He told Bilal to give up Islam.','But Bilal only said one word, again and again: "Ahad, Ahad." It means "One, One." He was saying that Allah is One, and nothing could make him stop believing.','Abu Bakr heard about Bilal and bought his freedom. Bilal was free at last.','Later, in Madinah, the Muslims needed a way to call everyone to prayer. The Prophet \ufdfa chose Bilal, because he had a strong and beautiful voice. Bilal became the very first person to call the adhan.','Every time you hear the adhan, you are hearing the same words Bilal called out more than 1,400 years ago.'],lesson:'Stay strong in what you believe, even when it is hard. Allah sees your patience.',question:'What word did Bilal keep saying, and what does it mean?'},{id:'abu-bakr',name:'Abu Bakr as-Siddiq',arabic:'\u0623\u0628\u0648 \u0628\u0643\u0631 \u0627\u0644\u0635\u062f\u064a\u0642',title:'The Best Friend',minutes:3,color:'#7A4E1D',paragraphs:['Abu Bakr was the closest friend of the Prophet \ufdfa. He was a kind and honest merchant in Makkah.','When the Prophet \ufdfa told people about the night journey to Jerusalem and up through the heavens, some people laughed. They said it was impossible.','But Abu Bakr said, "If he said it, then it is true." Because of this, he was called as-Siddiq, which means the one who always believes the truth.','When the Prophet \ufdfa had to leave Makkah for Madinah, Abu Bakr went with him. They hid in a cave called Thawr while their enemies searched for them.','Abu Bakr was scared for the Prophet \ufdfa. The Prophet \ufdfa told him gently, "Do not be sad. Allah is with us."'],lesson:'A true friend helps you do good and stands by you when things are scary.',question:'Why was Abu Bakr called as-Siddiq?'},{id:'umar',name:'Umar ibn al-Khattab',arabic:'\u0639\u0645\u0631 \u0628\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062e\u0637\u0627\u0628',title:'The Leader Who Carried the Flour',minutes:3,color:'#2E4A7A',paragraphs:['Umar became the leader of all the Muslims. Even though he was very important, he walked through the streets at night to check that everyone was okay.','A famous story tells that one night he heard children crying. He found a mother cooking a pot over a fire. But there was no food in the pot, only water. She was trying to calm her hungry children until they fell asleep.','Umar felt very sad. He went straight back and picked up a heavy sack of flour. His helper offered to carry it, but Umar said no. He wanted to carry it himself, because he felt responsible for the people.','He brought the food to the family and cooked for them until the children were full and smiling.'],lesson:'Being a leader means taking care of others, not just being in charge.',question:'Why did Umar want to carry the flour himself?'},{id:'uthman',name:'Uthman ibn Affan',arabic:'\u0639\u062b\u0645\u0627\u0646 \u0628\u0646 \u0639\u0641\u0627\u0646',title:'The Well of Water',minutes:2,color:'#1F6E8C',paragraphs:['In Madinah, there was a well called Rumah with fresh, sweet water. But the man who owned it made people pay for every bucket.','Many poor Muslims could not pay. Uthman was a rich man, and he wanted to help.','He bought the well and made the water free for everyone, forever. Rich or poor, anyone could come and drink.','Uthman was known for being very generous and very shy. He spent his money to help people again and again.'],lesson:'Sharing what Allah gave you is one of the best things you can do.',question:'What did Uthman do with the well?'},{id:'ali',name:'Ali ibn Abi Talib',arabic:'\u0639\u0644\u064a \u0628\u0646 \u0623\u0628\u064a \u0637\u0627\u0644\u0628',title:'The Brave Night',minutes:3,color:'#5A3A78',paragraphs:['Ali grew up in the home of the Prophet \ufdfa. He was one of the very first young people to become Muslim.','One night, enemies planned to hurt the Prophet \ufdfa. Allah told the Prophet \ufdfa to leave Makkah for Madinah.','Ali bravely slept in the Prophet\'s bed, under his cloak, so the enemies would think the Prophet \ufdfa was still there.','In the morning, the people of Makkah had left valuable things with the Prophet \ufdfa to keep safe, because everyone trusted him. Ali stayed behind and returned every single thing to its owner before he left too.'],lesson:'Be brave, and always give back what people trust you with.',question:'What did Ali do before he left Makkah?'},{id:'khadijah',name:'Khadijah bint Khuwaylid',arabic:'\u062e\u062f\u064a\u062c\u0629 \u0628\u0646\u062a \u062e\u0648\u064a\u0644\u062f',title:'The First to Believe',minutes:2,color:'#8C2F4A',paragraphs:['Khadijah was a smart and successful businesswoman in Makkah. She was the wife of the Prophet \ufdfa.','When the Prophet \ufdfa came home from the cave of Hira after the very first revelation, he was shaking and worried.','Khadijah wrapped him in a blanket and comforted him. She reminded him that he was kind to his family, helped the weak, and cared for guests, and that Allah would never let him down.','She was the very first person to believe in his message. She gave her time, her love and her wealth to support Islam.'],lesson:'Comfort the people you love, and remind them of the good in them.',question:'Who was the very first person to believe in the Prophet\'s message?'},{id:'musab',name:"Mus'ab ibn Umayr",arabic:'\u0645\u0635\u0639\u0628 \u0628\u0646 \u0639\u0645\u064a\u0631',title:'The First Teacher of Madinah',minutes:2,color:'#3E6B3A',paragraphs:["Mus'ab was a young man from a rich family. He wore the nicest clothes and the best perfume in all of Makkah.",'When he became Muslim, his family took away his comfortable life. He chose Islam anyway.','The Prophet \ufdfa sent him to Madinah to teach people about Islam. He was kind and patient, and he recited the Quran beautifully.',"So many people in Madinah became Muslim through Mus'ab that, by the time the Prophet \ufdfa arrived, the city was ready to welcome him."],lesson:'Kind words and patience can open people\'s hearts.',question:"Where did the Prophet \ufdfa send Mus'ab to teach?"},{id:'ibn-masud',name:"Abdullah ibn Mas'ud",arabic:'\u0639\u0628\u062f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0647 \u0628\u0646 \u0645\u0633\u0639\u0648\u062f',title:'The Boy Who Recited Out Loud',minutes:2,color:'#9A5B14',paragraphs:["Abdullah ibn Mas'ud was a young shepherd who looked after sheep. He was small and thin, but his heart was big.",'In the early days, Muslims recited the Quran quietly because the leaders of Makkah did not like it.','One day Abdullah walked right up to the Kaaba and recited Surah Ar-Rahman out loud for everyone to hear. Some people got angry and hurt him, but he was proud he had done it.','He became one of the best reciters of the Quran. The Prophet \ufdfa loved listening to him recite.'],lesson:'Learn the Quran well, and never be shy to share it.',question:'Which surah did Abdullah recite at the Kaaba?'}]},1371,[]);
